b5c0b95..249f72d preserves the reparam driver's output: the new Enzyme extension differentiates an IndexedReparametrization pair by pair, which agrees with the one-pass gradient to rounding but not bit for bit (3e-14 measured on a BRM-shaped correlated block), and the 23 paired driver runs are identical outside timing; the gradient timings predate the change and the BRM-harness rows were not re-run, so for those this record rests on the argument below, not on a measurement.

249f72d is 1336bb4 (covered by b5c0b95-1336bb4) plus the pairwise Enzyme gradient and the currency check's ext/ coverage. With a reverse-mode `AutoEnzyme` backend and an `IndexedReparametrization`, the new extension `ext/WarmupHMCEnzymeExt.jl` differentiates the per-gradient pass and the window-end halo transport with one `Enzyme.autodiff` per pair, in reverse pair order, instead of in one pass. That is the same arithmetic except for the order in which a coordinate read by several pairs sums their contributions, so it agrees with the one-pass gradient to rounding and is not bit for bit by construction (test/pairwise_enzyme_gradient.jl measures 1 ulp under runtime activity and 3e-14 on a correlated random-effect block). The driver's backend, `AutoEnzyme(; function_annotation = Enzyme.Const)`, reaches both extension methods in reverse mode (checked with `which` in the after side's benchmark environment), and every driver arm differentiates an `IndexedReparametrization`, so every run below went through the changed gradient. The paired reparam driver runs match outside timing and provenance. The rest of the change is the Enzyme weak dependency and extension declaration, and the currency check's `src/` + `ext/` comparison under docs/benchmark/.

Evidence: docs/benchmark/run_reparam_benchmark.jl with WHMC_BENCH_TARGETS=funnel,eight_schools-eight_schools_centered,seeds_data-seeds_centered_model,radon_mn-radon_partially_pooled_centered,radon_mn-radon_variable_intercept_centered, WHMC_BENCH_SEEDS=1 and WHMC_BENCH_DRAWS=1000 (WHMC_BENCH_AD unset): 23 runs per side, 0 failed. It was run on gordito on 2026-10-26 with Julia 1.10.12, at both clean WarmupHMC revisions, each in its own clone with docs/benchmark resolved against that clone through resolve_script. before/ is the same b5c0b95 run as b5c0b95-1336bb4/before/. before/ and after/ also keep the driver's gradient_overhead.json.

What this record does not show: the gradient-only probes (annotation_sweep, backend_replication, capture_boxing, prep_cost) and the driver's gradient_overhead.json time the pass this change replaces, so their figures predate it; the record covers them because the gradient they time is the same function to rounding, not because their timings were re-measured. The driver's own paired gradient_overhead.json measures the reparametrized gradient's cost over the bare one (live/bare) as 1.65 -> 1.58 (eight_schools), 1.11 -> 1.07 and 0.87 -> 1.01 (radon), 1.44 -> 1.15 (seeds) and 4.90 -> 4.66 (funnel), from single runs at different host load in which the bare gradient alone moved by 0.3x to 1.6x. For the other sampler harnesses (BRM inventory and catalogue, linear restart, nonlinear weighting, adaptive centering at fixed c) the claim rests on the same argument: the change can move a trajectory only through a last-bit difference in a gradient, and none appeared in the 23 driver runs. That is weakest for the BRM harnesses: their correlated blocks are where the test does measure a difference, through the window-end transport (BRM supplies its own per-gradient pass), so a re-run there may differ in late digits; it was not re-run for this record.
